# Architecture-Tracked Project: {{projectName}}

This project uses a living architecture document, `{{architectureFile}}`. You — the AI coding agent (Codex, OpenCode, Copilot, or any AGENTS.md-aware tool) — are responsible for keeping it in sync with the code.

> **arkive is installed.** MCP server available via `npx @ericjdz/arkive serve --stdio`. Use MCP tools when connected; edit `{{architectureFile}}` directly when not.

## The Two Rules

1. **Structure is derived. You author intent.** The module tree, the third-party packages, and the import graph are produced by static analysis (`npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply`). You record what the code cannot say: `summary`, `owner`, `stack`, `dataFlows`, and decisions with their rationale and rejected alternatives.
2. **Read the document for context, never as an authority over the code.** When it and the code disagree, the code is right — run `reconcile --apply` and take the derived half from the scan.

## Major Changes

Add/remove a service, datastore, or external API · change communication protocols · modify infrastructure · adopt a new framework · refactor module boundaries.

**Before any major change**, re-read `{{architectureFile}}` to learn what exists and why.

**After any major change**: run `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply`, append a `history` entry, and record the decision if one was made.

## How to Update

**Derive structure:** `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply` — writes `nodes[]` and `edges[]` from the code with `rule` + `evidence` on every element.
**Author intent:** `update_architecture` (MCP) or edit `{{architectureFile}}` directly — `summary`, `owner`, `stack`, `dataFlows`, and `decisions`.
**Verify:** `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile` reports drift without writing.

Do NOT hand-transcribe the module tree, the dependency list, or the import graph. A transcribed edge is a claim nothing checks, and it drifts from the first commit.

## Schema (compact)

- **Node**: `{ id, kind, name, summary, provenance }`
  - `kind`: `module | service | datastore | external | datastructure | endpoint`
  - `tech`, `path`, `ports`, `owner`, `deletedAt` are optional.
- **Edge**: `{ id, from, to, kind, provenance }`
  - `kind`: `imports | calls | reads | writes | derives_from`. `decided_by` is computed from `decision.subjects` and is never stored. `label` is transport naming only, never semantic.
- **Provenance** (required on every node and edge, exactly one shape):
  - `{ "derived": true, "rule": "<rule-id>", "evidence": ["file:line"] }`
  - `{ "derived": false, "authoredBy": "agent | human", "reviewedAt": "<ISO 8601>" }`
  - **No `confidence` field exists.** It was declared and read but written by no code path; a number an LLM assigns to itself is not evidence. `evidence: ["file:line"]` is.
- **Decision**: `{ id, topic, decision, rationale, alternatives[{name, whyNot}], timestamp, subjects[{kind: "node|edge", ref}] }`
  - MUST include at least one rejected alternative with a non-empty `whyNot`.
  - `subjects` names nodes *and* edges.
- **DataFlow**: `{ name, steps[{actor, action}] }`
- **History entry**: `{ timestamp, action, summary, agent? }`

The document's discriminator is `"schemaVersion": 2`. There is no v1 `version` string, and a document carrying one is a v1 file this build refuses to write over.

`services[]` and `connections[]` may appear in the file. They are a **derived view computed from `nodes[]` / `edges[]` on every read and rewritten from the graph on every write.** Never write to them; anything you put there is replaced on the next write without a warning.

## Validation Rules

- Node, edge, and decision ids are unique. Every `edge.from`/`to` names an existing node.
- Every derived element cites `evidence` with a `file:line` someone can open.
- Every decision must include at least one rejected alternative with `whyNot`.
- A decision whose `subjects` no longer resolve is reported, never deleted.
- All timestamps are ISO 8601. `project.lastUpdated` is set on every write.

## CLI Commands

- `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile --apply` — derive structure from the code and write it
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive reconcile` — report drift without writing anything
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive watch` — re-derive on every change
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive serve` — MCP server + web viewer
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive serve --stdio` — MCP server only (agent integration)
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive status` — counts, schema version, drift verdict
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive export <markdown|mermaid>` — export
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive learn` — web viewer in Learning Mode
- `npx @ericjdz/arkive install <platform>` — register MCP server in your AI platform config

## MCP Auto-Install

To register the arkive MCP server in your platform, ask the user which platform they use, then run:

```bash
npx @ericjdz/arkive install <platform>
```

Supported platforms: `claude-desktop`, `claude-code`, `cursor`, `opencode`, `vscode`, `windsurf`, `cline`

The command writes the correct config entry for the chosen platform. The user must restart their agent session afterward.